# The `window` Object

JavaScript and the browser give us many built-in objects, and arguably the most important is **`window`**. It represents the browser window itself, and it also serves as the **global context** for everything that happens on the page. It is the top-level global object.

`window` has properties that tell us about the user's browsing context:

- `window.innerWidth`: the width of the window's content area, in pixels.
- `window.innerHeight`: the height of the window's content area, in pixels.
- `window.location`: an object describing the URL the browser is on. `window.location.href` gives that URL as a string.

```{javascript autorun}
console.log(window.location.href);
console.log(window.innerWidth);
console.log(window.innerHeight);
```

Resize the browser window and run that cell again. The numbers change, because these properties always report the window's current state.

It also has built-in methods:

- `window.alert(message)` displays a modal dialog that the user has to dismiss by clicking OK.
- `window.prompt(message)` displays a modal dialog with an input field, and returns whatever the user typed.

These two interrupt the page, so the cells below are not set to run automatically. Run them yourself when you want to see them.

```{javascript}
alert('This is a window alert!');
```

```{javascript}
const name = prompt('What is your name?');

console.log(name);
```

Type `Steve` into the second one and click OK, and `name` holds the string `Steve`. `prompt` hands back the text as its return value, which is why we store it in a variable.

## Omitting `window.`

Here is the consequence of `window` being the top-level global object: **all global variables and functions are automatically properties of `window`**. Which means we can leave off the `window.` prefix whenever we use one.

```{javascript autorun}
console.log(window.innerWidth === innerWidth);
console.log(window.location.href === location.href);
```

So `alert('hi')` and `window.alert('hi')` are the same call, and `innerWidth` and `window.innerWidth` are the same value. You will see both styles in real code. The short form is more common.

## `document`

One property of `window` is important enough to get its own lesson: **`window.document`**, or just `document`. It represents the HTML document currently loaded in the browser, and it is the entry point to the DOM, which is how JavaScript reads and changes the content of the page.

```{javascript autorun}
console.log(window.document === document);
```

That is the subject of the next lesson.

## Exercises

1. Read two properties of `window`. Store the page's URL as a string in `pageUrl`, and the window's inner width in `windowWidth`. Read the width *without* the `window.` prefix, to prove that its properties are global.

    ```{javascript}
    const pageUrl = /* Your code here */;
    const windowWidth = /* Your code here */;

    console.log(pageUrl, windowWidth);
    ```
    ```+solution
    const pageUrl = window.location.href;
    const windowWidth = innerWidth;

    console.log(pageUrl, windowWidth);
    ```
    ```+test
    wrap(pageUrl).should('equal', window.location.href).run('`pageUrl` should hold the URL string from `window.location.href`', 'Read the page URL');
    wrap(windowWidth).should('equal', window.innerWidth).run('`windowWidth` should be the window\'s inner width', 'Read the window width');
    wrap(/(^|[^.\w])innerWidth/.test(source)).should('equal', true).run('Read `innerWidth` without the `window.` prefix', 'Used the global form');
    ```

2. Write a function `windowSize` that returns an object describing the window right now, with a `width` property holding the inner width and a `height` property holding the inner height. Read both without the `window.` prefix.

    ```{javascript}
    function windowSize() {
      // Write your code here
    }

    console.log(windowSize());
    ```
    ```+solution
    function windowSize() {
      return { width: innerWidth, height: innerHeight };
    }

    console.log(windowSize());
    ```
    ```+test
    wrap(windowSize().width).should('equal', window.innerWidth).run('The returned object should have a `width` property holding the inner width', 'Reported the width');
    wrap(windowSize().height).should('equal', window.innerHeight).run('The returned object should have a `height` property holding the inner height', 'Reported the height');
    wrap(/(^|[^.\w])innerHeight/.test(source)).should('equal', true).run('Read `innerHeight` without the `window.` prefix', 'Used the global form');
    ```

3. Why the `window.` prefix is optional

    ```{mcq}
    `alert('hi')` and `window.alert('hi')` do the same thing. Why can the `window.` prefix be left off when calling `alert`?
    correct: Correct! `window` is the top-level global object, so every global name is already a property of it, and the prefix is optional.
    wrong: Look back at the "Omitting `window.`" section.
    [x] `window` is the global object, so its properties are also global names
    [ ] `alert` is a special case that the browser handles differently from other `window` methods
    [ ] The browser copies every `window` method into `document` when the page loads
    [ ] Leaving the prefix off creates a new function that calls `window.alert` for you
    ```

